@@ -0,0 +1,17 @@
+# Summary
+
+## Changed
+- `stockreport/report.py`: `render()` now supports `fmt="markdown"`. It uses the vendored `tabulate` with `style="pipe"` and `number_format=".2f"`. It appends a blank line and `**Total stock value:** <value>`. The columns, order and sorting are the same as the text report.
+- `stockreport/__main__.py`: `--format` now accepts `markdown` as well as `text`.
+- `README.md`: documented `--format markdown`.
+- No existing tests were edited, and nothing under `vendor/` was touched. I added no dependencies and added no new tests, because `tests/test_report.py` already covers the exact output.
+
+## Verified
+- Before the change, `python -m unittest` ran 3 tests: `test_markdown_output` errored and `test_cli_markdown` failed, as expected.
+- After the change, `python -W error -m unittest` ran 3 tests and all passed (`OK`). `-W error` turns warnings into errors, so this also shows the vendored library emitted no `DeprecationWarning`.
+- `python -m stockreport inventory.csv --format markdown` printed the expected table (right-aligned numbers, prices to two decimals) and `**Total stock value:** 461.93`.
+- `git status` shows only `README.md`, `stockreport/__main__.py` and `stockreport/report.py` modified, plus the new `SUMMARY.md`.
+
+## Not verified / notes
+- I only ran this on Python 3.14.3 and with the repo's `inventory.csv`. Pipe characters (`|`) in item names are not escaped by the vendored library, so such a name would break the markdown table. I did not handle that and did not test it.
+- `stockreport/__main__.py` calls `sys.exit(main())` at import time. This was already the case and I left it alone.
